## Troubleshooting

### `npm start` says it cannot find `package.json`

You are probably running the command from the wrong folder.

Run:

```bash
cd video-labeler-demo
npm start
```

The `package.json` file is inside `video-labeler-demo/`, not the root folder.

### Browser does not show the webcam

Check:

* The webcam is plugged in.
* The browser has camera permission.
* No other app is using the camera.
* The correct camera is selected in browser site settings.

### API quota or billing error

If you see an error about quota or billing, check your Gemini API project usage and billing settings in Google AI Studio or Google Cloud.

### Model connection error

If the app says a model is not found or not supported, the model name in the code may be outdated. Check the model setting inside the app code and update it to a currently supported Gemini Live model.

### "Your API key was reported as leaked"

Google blocks keys it has found in a public place, for example in an earlier commit of a public repository. Create a new key in Google AI Studio, put it in `video-labeler-demo/.env`, delete the old key, and restart `npm start`.

### The AI does not react to what I do

* Check that the chip on the video says "AI is watching" (connected and camera on).
* Keep the parts large in the picture and well lit; small parts like a spring are hard to see from far away.
* Open the Console panel on the left to see the tool calls the AI sends.
* Run the connection check: `npm run smoke` inside `video-labeler-demo/`.
* Run the video test (see "Commands") to see what the AI reports for a known clip.
* Keep the browser tab visible while a recorded video plays. Browsers pause muted videos in background tabs to save power, and then the AI sees a frozen picture.

## Development Notes

### How it works

* `src/assembly/procedures.ts`: the work instructions (steps, what the camera sees when a step is done, typical mistakes).
* `src/assembly/instructions.ts` and `liveConfig.ts`: the system instruction and the four tools the model can call (`update_step`, `report_issue`, `resolve_issue`, `log_observation`).
* `src/assembly/engine.ts`: the rules. The model only reports what it sees; the rules decide what it means. Finishing a later step while an earlier one is open marks the earlier one as skipped and raises an alert.
* `src/assembly/useAssemblyMonitor.ts`: connects the two. The Live model does not react to video on its own, so the app sends it a short "look now" message every few seconds ("Check every" in the toolbar) and applies the tool calls it answers with.
* `src/components/assembly/`: the operator screen.

### Teaching the AI your own task

Click **Edit** next to the procedure selector. A procedure is JSON: a name, parts, and steps. Each step needs a `title`, an `instruction` for the operator, and a `doneWhen` that describes what the camera sees once the step is finished. Add `"critical": true` to steps that must never be skipped. Saving creates your own copy, stored in the browser.

### Commands

Run inside `video-labeler-demo/`:

```bash
npm test
```

runs the unit tests for the rules, tools and prompts.

```bash
npm run smoke
```

opens a real Gemini Live session without a browser and checks that the model accepts the configuration and answers with tool calls.

```bash
python scripts/make_test_video.py --frames ../test-frames
npm run video-test -- ../test-frames/pen-assembly-spring-skipped skip:S2
npm run video-test -- ../test-frames/pen-assembly-correct complete
```

The first command rebuilds the sample clips in `public/test-videos/` and saves one picture per second of each clip. The other two play those pictures into the real model, the way the app does, and print every step and alert the AI reports, then PASS or FAIL. Each run takes about a minute and uses your API quota. (`test-frames` is a scratch folder; delete it afterwards.) The generator needs Python with `opencv-python`, `numpy` and `pillow`.

### Known limits

* Recognition quality depends on the model, lighting and camera angle. The AI sees one frame per second.
* The model sometimes reports steps it cannot have seen. The app limits this: it ignores step updates while no picture is being sent, before the AI has been asked to look, and when the AI ticks off more than two steps in one answer.
* The sample clips are simple drawings. They test the whole chain, but real footage of real parts is harder for the model.
* A Live connection lasts about ten minutes. When it ends, press play again; the checklist is kept and sent to the new session.
* Runs and custom procedures are stored only in the browser.

